///sha-256;qCDGD6GffNUF3AWop3yYAYQF67unUC-PHehcLIRDcIMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2026-07-20T13:30:44.613Zƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;rEboyHpc8RFne7PBBR3AG48u75xVNLPw2HNfe-501x0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx|https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/jul/20/yellow-pine-by-claire-vaye-watkins-review-messy-portrait-of-protest-subcultureƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;FjyZO9755Ea6TVI3-4xdfOA78fPX48YmAAElqCp-3e4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9https://newscable-source-broker.invalid/activitypub/actorƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;IQ7NIlBrKaN7YYg6nIISRbsHq0ZDpJikaoYrYII60eAƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;URjdOYsu6ueEG5ci-0olpLeV9ChjI0WlQxvJHjylhC0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx|https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/jul/20/yellow-pine-by-claire-vaye-watkins-review-messy-portrait-of-protest-subcultureƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;TL44vUNpcmYZP40F339Mpb3eDxba4jgWG6s62IBSE7cƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky<p>Watkins should have been the ideal writer for this sympathetic take on new age spirituality and desert eco-activism â€“ but the results are confusing</p><p>Rose, the protagonist of Claire Vaye Watkinsâ€™s latest novel, has retreated from the world to live in Nothingness Flats, a cinder-block cabin in the Nevada desert. On indefinite leave from her job at an environmental organisation, Rose works on her Al-Anon recovery, goes hiking and joins protests against the Yellow Pine of the title: a solar array being built over a fragile desert ecosystem. She writes in her journal: â€œI am on this side of the portal. I have compassion in my heart for anyone back on the other side, sending emails, bickering, buying useless shit, getting run by the algorithm, living in the past.â€ As the story begins, she is reunited with her first love, Miles, who has â€œfallen apart beautifully: bopped in and out of law school, then spent six years in whatâ€™s left of the redwoods reading horny 400-year-old poems before bailing on his PhDâ€. Soon, Rose realises what she truly wants, this side of the portal, is to have his child.</p><p>You donâ€™t often see a sympathetic take on the subculture of 21st-century hippies, with their eclectic leftwing philosophies, new age spirituality and earnest striving to do good and be kind. Itâ€™s an important strain in modern life, and one typically dismissed with facile mockery and condescension. As a serious treatment of it, Yellow Pine has notable strengths. The love story between Rose and Miles is very real and specific to this world, and gives a&nbsp;backbone of plot to material that otherwise tends to sprawl. Watkinsâ€™s prose can be musical and keenly attentive, and she excels at conveying starry-eyed love. â€œ[Miles] was brilliant, peaceful, grounded as a mountain, kind even when he was blue, an unabashed loner agog at the whole burning world.â€</p> <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/jul/20/yellow-pine-by-claire-vaye-watkins-review-messy-portrait-of-protest-subculture">Continue reading...</a>ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;if88dQTbj-Mq3xW8A6q0rdGr8uGPXSI3v7rFM499G68ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;OM01gN0_y6YQgAUBRCbtrcwNjnzHlDVyG7lZN6R2raAƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;-nZINCqDEy1AQwtH9Jf5UrCOY0LjTONXfiOjbe1_hYIƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEnti